// result_begin: markdown
First40 fixed-bank samples; no gallery selection.
\image(figures/self_attention_gan_generated.png)
// result_end

\caption(Five rows of eight generated images retain the original pixel display range[0,1].)
\alttext(Mostly grey weak generated images appear in the fixed first-forty gallery.)

> The coarse diagnostic has a deliberately visible blind spot

The MMD diagnostic first averages each 7×7 pixel block, giving 16 coordinates per image,
and divides those coordinates by 4. It then averages IMQ kernels with constants 1,4,16
and excludes within-pool diagonals in the unbiased two-sample estimate. This statistic
can be negative at finite sample size and discards fine digit structure. The baseline
repeats the fitting-only mean image 200 times; it uses no held-out pixels to construct it.
Its much smaller value shows why coarse MMD alone cannot certify sample quality or
coverage. Neither the mean nor MMD adds a fitting objective or selects the checkpoint.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/self_attention_gan_mean.png", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
